red.add_paragraph('The following blocks show the material draft language and proposed seller-side replacement. Conforming provisions are documented in the full-document disposition checklist.')

block(red,'C-1','CRITICAL','Section 3.1 — Standard of Performance','“at least equal to or better than” the level provided during the twenty-four (24) month period prior to Closing, and “in all cases in accordance with industry best practices”; sufficient resources at all times.','Service Provider shall perform each Service in a manner substantially consistent with the manner and level of quality at which such Service (or substantially similar service) was provided to or on behalf of the Business during the twelve (12) months immediately preceding the Closing Date. Historical practices, methodologies, systems and resource levels shall be the benchmark. Service Provider shall not be required to exceed that historical level, prioritize the Services over its retained businesses, or adopt external industry standards, best practices, new methodologies or systems not then used for the Business.','APA §§7.12(a), 7.12(f); Playbook §2.1.','The current standard creates an objective ratchet and an undefined external benchmark. It can require Polaris to improve service levels and prioritize Trident over retained divisions.','Firm must-change. “Commercially reasonable efforts” may be discussed only as a fallback after the historical benchmark is preserved.','Replace the 24-month lookback and “equal to or better” / best-practices language. This is a direct APA conflict and material operational risk.','APA §§7.12(a), 7.12(f)')
block(red,'C-2','CRITICAL','Section 5.2 — Automatic Renewal','Automatic renewal for successive six-month periods, up to two Renewal Terms, unless Polaris gives non-renewal notice 120 days before expiration.','Delete Section 5.2 in its entirety. The Agreement and each Service shall expire no later than eighteen (18) months after the Closing Date, unless the Parties mutually agree in a written amendment to extend an individual Service for no more than an additional six (6) months. No automatic renewal or extension shall apply. Any extension remains subject to the APA and applicable cost-plus cap.','APA §7.12(a); Playbook §3.1.','The TSA can run 30 months and renewal occurs by inaction, depriving Polaris of an exit and conflicting with the APA hard stop.','Firm must-change. No automatic renewal; any extension requires mutual written agreement.','Delete the automatic-renewal mechanism. The APA’s 10% cap controls any extension even though the playbook target is cost-plus-15%.','APA §7.12(a)')
block(red,'C-3','CRITICAL','Section 5.3 — Termination of Individual Services','Either Party may terminate an individual Service upon not less than one hundred twenty (120) days’ prior written notice; Fees cease on the effective date.','Either Party may terminate any individual Service upon exactly ninety (90) days’ prior written notice. Buyer shall pay Fees accrued through the effective date and reimburse Seller for non-cancelable costs or commitments incurred before receipt of the notice in reasonable reliance on continuation of the Service, subject to commercially reasonable mitigation.','APA §7.12(c).','The 120-day period locks Polaris into service obligations longer than the APA permits and omits the non-cancelable-cost protection.','Firm must-change. The 90-day period is both the minimum and maximum under the APA.','Change 120 days to 90 days and restore APA cost reimbursement.','APA §7.12(c)')
block(red,'C-4','CRITICAL','Sections 6.1 and 6.4 / Schedule G — Fee methodology and true-up','Fees are sole compensation and fixed for the duration of the Term; no escalation or adjustment except as expressly provided. Schedule G fixes monthly fees and prohibits additional charges without Buyer approval.','Fees are estimated monthly charges based on Fully-Loaded Cost plus a markup not exceeding 10% of applicable Fully-Loaded Cost. Fees shall be adjusted as necessary to reflect actual Fully-Loaded Costs incurred during the Transition Period, calculated consistently with the APA. No category may exceed the applicable Exhibit H estimate by more than 5% without Buyer’s prior written consent. Reasonable direct out-of-pocket and third-party costs incurred in providing Services may be invoiced as included in, or in addition to, estimated monthly charges to the extent consistent with the APA and applicable Schedule.','APA §7.12(b); Exhibit H; Playbook §§4.1–4.2.','Fixed-fee language may prevent actual cost recovery and is inconsistent with the APA’s actual-cost adjustment. “All-in” wording shifts unbudgeted third-party costs to Polaris.','Firm on APA conformity and 10% cap; mechanics and documentation are negotiable.','Replace fixed-fee language with actual-cost adjustment while preserving the APA’s 5% estimate threshold and 10% per-category cap.','APA §7.12(b)')
block(red,'C-5','CRITICAL','Section 10.1 — Aggregate Liability Cap','Polaris’s aggregate liability shall not exceed two hundred percent (200%) of total Service Charges actually paid under the Agreement as of the claim date.','Polaris’s aggregate liability under or in connection with this Agreement shall not exceed the total TSA Fees actually paid by Buyer to Seller during the twelve (12) month period immediately preceding the date the applicable claim is first asserted in writing. During the first twelve (12) months after Closing, the cap equals the TSA Fees actually paid from Closing through the claim-assertion date. The cap applies in the aggregate and includes Polaris’s Affiliates, officers, directors, employees and agents. Carve-outs shall be limited to those required by APA §7.12(d), including fraud or willful misconduct, confidentiality breaches and qualifying third-party indemnity claims.','APA §7.12(d); Playbook §5.1.','Using the draft’s as-written monthly total of $1,139,000, an 18-month run produces $20,502,000 and a $41,004,000 cap. Correcting IT to the APA-compliant 10% markup yields $1,122,000 per month; the APA rolling 12-month cap is $13,464,000. Full-term delta is about $27,540,000 (3.05x). At month 12, the as-written draft cap is about $27,336,000 versus $13,464,000.','Firm must-change. The cap is expressly mandated by the APA and is not a negotiation point.','Replace the total-fees multiple with the trailing 12-month fees-actually-paid formulation. Figures are illustrative and vary with actual duration and costs.','APA §7.12(d)')
block(red,'C-6','CRITICAL','Section 10.2 — Consequential Damages','“SERVICE PROVIDER HEREBY WAIVES” all consequential, incidental, special, punitive, exemplary and indirect damages against Service Recipient.','NEITHER PARTY SHALL BE LIABLE TO THE OTHER FOR CONSEQUENTIAL, INCIDENTAL, INDIRECT, SPECIAL, EXEMPLARY OR PUNITIVE DAMAGES, INCLUDING LOST PROFITS, LOST REVENUE, LOSS OF BUSINESS OPPORTUNITY, DIMINUTION IN VALUE, LOSS OF GOODWILL OR COST OF REPLACEMENT SERVICES, ARISING OUT OF OR RELATING TO THIS AGREEMENT, REGARDLESS OF THEORY OR NOTICE OF POSSIBILITY; PROVIDED THAT THIS SENTENCE SHALL NOT LIMIT A PARTY’S LIABILITY FOR THIRD-PARTY CLAIMS FOR WHICH INDEMNIFICATION IS PROVIDED UNDER THIS AGREEMENT.','APA §7.12(d); Playbook §5.2.','The unilateral waiver leaves Polaris exposed to Trident’s claims for business interruption, lost contracts, supply-chain disruption and other consequential loss.','Firm must-change. Never accept a non-mutual waiver.','Make the waiver mutual and preserve the APA’s third-party indemnity carve-out.','APA §7.12(d)')
block(red,'C-7','CRITICAL','Section 7.1 — Service Provider Materials / IP','Perpetual, irrevocable, worldwide, royalty-free license to use, reproduce, modify, adapt and create derivative works of all Service Provider Materials, with sublicense rights surviving termination.','Delete the license grant. All Service Provider Materials, including tools, methodologies, templates, processes, software, systems, know-how and proprietary technology, remain Polaris’s sole and exclusive property. No license, sublicense or other right is granted. During the Term only, Trident may access the Materials solely to receive the Services and may not copy, modify, reverse engineer, distribute, sublicense or create derivative works. Access ends immediately at expiration or termination, subject only to a separate written license.','Playbook §6.1; client instructions.','The provision exports enterprise-wide operational IP across all four divisions on a perpetual, irrevocable and royalty-free basis, creating competitive leakage and board-level risk.','Firm, non-negotiable. Any post-transition access requires a separate, restricted, priced license.','Delete the entire license and substitute a no-license reservation with access only during the service term.','Playbook §6.1')
block(red,'C-8','CRITICAL','Sections 8.4 and new 8.5 — Mexico data privacy and IMMEX','Each Party shall comply only with applicable United States privacy laws. Schedule E merely states that the Parties will include “appropriate provisions” for IMMEX.','Each Party shall comply with all applicable data-protection laws, expressly including Mexico’s Federal Law on Protection of Personal Data Held by Private Parties (LFPDPPP) and its Regulations. For Monterrey employee data, Trident is the controller and Polaris is the processor. The Parties shall implement or attach a data-processing addendum covering purpose limitation, documented instructions, privacy notices (avisos de privacidad), required consents or other lawful transfer mechanisms, security, subprocessors, breach notice to Trident within 48 hours after awareness, data-subject assistance, return/deletion and lawful retention. Trident is responsible for controller notices, lawful basis and employee-facing disclosures; Polaris shall process only for the Services.

New Section 8.5 — IMMEX. Trident, as post-Closing operator of the Monterrey Facility, has primary responsibility for maintaining or obtaining IMMEX certification and complying with Mexican customs and trade requirements, including temporary-import records and Secretaría de Economía filings. Polaris shall provide specified transition assistance and reasonably cooperate with transfer, renewal and reporting activities, but is not responsible for Trident’s failure to obtain or maintain post-Closing certification. Each Party shall notify the other within two Business Days of an event that could reasonably jeopardize certification or create a customs violation; the Parties shall cooperate on remediation.','APA §7.12(g); Playbook §§6.3 and 12; client instructions regarding 300+ Mexican employees and IMMEX.','U.S.-only language leaves no contractual allocation for LFPDPPP compliance, cross-border transfers, breach response or retention. The placeholder leaves unclear who maintains certification and who bears customs/tax exposure if it is jeopardized.','Firm must-change. Local Mexico counsel should confirm implementation, but the TSA must contain substantive allocation before signature.','Insert substantive provisions and attach a short data-processing addendum. Keep Buyer as primary operator and limit Polaris liability to its own breach / gross negligence / willful misconduct under the agreed caps.','APA §7.12(g)')
block(red,'S-1','SIGNIFICANT','Section 4.3 and Schedule H — Key Personnel / staffing discretion','Polaris may not reassign, transfer, terminate or remove named Key Personnel without Trident’s prior written consent; replacements require Trident approval and FTE allocations are minimum commitments.','Polaris shall retain sole discretion over hiring, assignment, reassignment, promotion, transfer, replacement and termination of personnel providing Services. Polaris shall maintain reasonably qualified personnel sufficient to meet the Service Standard. For personnel on an agreed contact list, Polaris shall provide 15 Business Days’ advance notice of a planned reassignment or replacement where practicable and designate a reasonably qualified replacement, but no Trident consent is required. Schedule H and FTE percentages are planning estimates, not minimum commitments.','Playbook §7.1; APA §7.12(e).','Buyer consent impairs shared-services workforce management and may force Polaris to retain personnel solely for Trident.','Firm target. Notice and qualified-replacement obligations are reasonable fallbacks; buyer consent is not acceptable.','Remove consent rights and minimum FTE commitments; make Schedule H non-binding.','Playbook §7.1')
block(red,'S-2','SIGNIFICANT','Sections 9.1–9.2 — Indemnification','Polaris indemnifies Trident for any breach of any representation, warranty, covenant or obligation and any violation of Applicable Law; indemnities are not limited to third-party claims or the cap.','Trident shall indemnify Polaris for third-party claims arising from Trident’s use of or reliance on Services, Trident’s breach, or negligent / wrongful acts of Trident personnel, except to the extent caused by Polaris’s gross negligence or willful misconduct. Polaris’s indemnity is limited to third-party claims to the extent caused by Polaris’s gross negligence or willful misconduct in performing Services. All indemnification is subject to Section 10.1, except only to the extent expressly carved out by APA §7.12(d).','Playbook §5.3; APA §7.12(d).','The draft converts ordinary breaches and any statutory violation into uncapped first-party indemnity claims against Polaris.','Firm target. Proportionate-fault language can be a fallback, but provider indemnity must remain third-party and gross-negligence / willful-misconduct based.','Rewrite both indemnities to address third-party claims and apply the cap.','Playbook §5.3')
block(red,'S-3','SIGNIFICANT','New Section 5.7 — Termination Assistance / wind-down','No express termination-assistance or wind-down obligation; Section 5.5 ends all rights and obligations immediately.','Upon expiration or termination of a Service, Polaris may provide reasonable termination assistance for up to 60 days, limited to knowledge transfer, data migration support, cooperation with replacement providers and transition documentation. Assistance is separately invoiced at the applicable cost-plus rate permitted by the APA and is not required while Trident has unpaid undisputed amounts. Polaris has no obligation beyond the period or to maintain personnel, systems or third-party arrangements solely for that purpose.','Playbook §3.4; APA §7.12(e).','Silence invites claims of indefinite post-termination cooperation. Unpriced wind-down would extend the operational burden after the service ends.','Include the boundary and compensation. 60 days is target; up to 90 days and pricing can be negotiated, but APA markup cap controls.','Add a defined wind-down right / obligation conditioned on payment and available resources.','Playbook §3.4')
block(red,'S-4','SIGNIFICANT','New Section 2.5 — Change orders','No formal process for out-of-scope services, volume increases or scope modifications.','No out-of-scope Service, material volume increase, enhancement or scope change shall be performed unless documented in a written change order signed by authorized representatives of both Parties. Acceptance is at Polaris’s sole discretion. The order states scope, assumptions, schedule, fees, applicable markup (not exceeding the APA cap unless APA expressly permits otherwise) and any advance payment. Polaris has no obligation until execution.','Playbook §4.4; APA §7.12(b).','Without a change-order gate, Trident can expand scope informally and later argue the work is covered by the monthly fee.','Firm on written dual signature, seller discretion and no work before execution. Pricing follows the APA cap.','Insert a new Section 2.5 and cross-reference it in each Schedule’s assumptions.','Playbook §4.4')
block(red,'S-5','SIGNIFICANT','Section 6.4 / Schedule G — escalation','Fees remain fixed for the Initial Term and any Renewal Terms, with no escalation or adjustment.','Fees may be adjusted on each anniversary of Closing to reflect actual Fully-Loaded Costs and material cost increases from a Change in Law, vendor pricing or material volume / scope change, subject to the APA’s 10% markup cap and Exhibit H estimate / 5% consent threshold. Material scope or volume increases use the change-order procedure.','Playbook §4.2; APA §7.12(b).','Fixed pricing can turn the TSA into a below-market subsidy and leaves Polaris bearing inflation and vendor increases.','Mechanics negotiable; preserve actual-cost recovery and the APA cap.','Replace the fixed-fee sentence with a compliant adjustment mechanism; avoid a standalone 3% floor that could violate the APA cap.','Playbook §4.2')
block(red,'S-6','SIGNIFICANT','Section 6.3 — Payment terms','Buyer shall pay undisputed invoices within a “commercially reasonable time.”','Buyer shall pay each undisputed invoice within 30 days after receipt. Late amounts bear interest at the lesser of 1.5% per month or the legal maximum. Undisputed portions are due regardless of disputes concerning other portions. Polaris may suspend the affected Service after an undisputed invoice remains unpaid more than 60 days after written notice.','Playbook §4.3.','Vague timing undermines cash-flow predictability and makes non-payment difficult to enforce.','Firm target; Net 45 is maximum fallback.','Replace vague timing with Net 30, interest and suspension / termination protection.','Playbook §4.3')
block(red,'S-7','SIGNIFICANT','Section 14 — Audit rights','Trident may audit up to twice per calendar year, at Polaris’s expense, on 10 Business Days’ notice, including systems, facilities, personnel and performance records.','Trident may audit fee-related books and records no more than once in any twelve-month period, at Trident’s sole expense, on 30 Business Days’ notice. Audits are during normal business hours and exclude proprietary systems, internal cost methodologies and unrelated personnel records. An independent auditor must be confidential. If an audit shows an overcharge exceeding 5% for the audited period, Polaris credits the overcharge and reasonable audit costs; otherwise Trident bears all costs.','Playbook §9.','The draft invites disruptive audits, shifts all cost to Polaris and exposes enterprise systems beyond fee verification.','Firm target; twice per TSA term and 20 days notice are fallback.','Replace the broad audit clause with a buyer-paid, fee-records-only regime and 5% threshold.','Playbook §9')
block(red,'S-8','SIGNIFICANT','Section 12.1 — Force majeure exit','Force majeure suspends performance but contains no termination trigger for a prolonged event.','If a Force Majeure Event materially prevents performance of an affected Service for 90 consecutive days, either Party may terminate that Service on written notice without further liability for nonperformance after termination. Fees are adjusted to services actually delivered; accrued payment obligations survive.','Playbook §10.','Without an exit right, Polaris can remain tied to an unavailable or obsolete service indefinitely.','Firm target; 120 consecutive days is maximum fallback.','Add the 90-day trigger and payment / adjustment mechanics.','Playbook §10')
block(red,'S-9','SIGNIFICANT','Section 13 — insurance','Trident maintains only $2M CGL; Polaris maintains $5M CGL, workers’ compensation and $5M E&O for 12 months after termination; no additional-insured or waiver-of-subrogation protection.','Trident shall maintain through the Term and for 12 months thereafter: CGL of at least $5M per occurrence and aggregate, $5M umbrella / excess and statutory workers’ compensation. Polaris is an additional insured under CGL and umbrella, with waiver of subrogation in Polaris’s favor; certificates are due within 10 Business Days after Closing and annually. Polaris shall maintain only insurance customarily maintained for comparable operations and coverage required by law, with no incremental or extended coverage solely for the TSA.','Playbook §8; industrial / chemical manufacturing risk.','Buyer’s $2M-only requirement is inadequate and gives Polaris no direct policy rights. The draft imposes unnecessary procurement burdens on Polaris.','$5M limits are target; $3M is minimum fallback. AI and waiver are firm.','Upgrade Trident coverage and reduce Polaris’s affirmative procurement burden.','Playbook §8')
block(red,'S-10','SIGNIFICANT','Sections 15.1–15.2 — law and dispute resolution','Ohio law; exclusive litigation in Cuyahoga County, Ohio; jury waiver.','Pennsylvania law governs. Disputes first escalate to CFO / General Counsel representatives for 15 Business Days, followed by optional non-binding mediation. Unresolved disputes are finally resolved by confidential AAA commercial arbitration before one arbitrator in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.','Playbook §11.','Buyer-home-state litigation creates home-court and public-docket risk and loses arbitration’s confidentiality, limited discovery and industry expertise.','Pennsylvania / AAA / Pittsburgh is target. Neutral arbitration or three arbitrators for disputes above $5M may be fallback; litigation is not preferred.','Replace Ohio law and court litigation with Pennsylvania law and arbitration.','Playbook §11')
